/*

        One should not abandon duties born of one’s nature, even if one sees defects in
        them, O son of Kunti. Indeed, all endeavors are veiled by some flaw, as fire is
        by smoke.

                              Bhagavad Gita: Chapter 18, Verse 48


    This file is different from the previous ones. It does not describe a language feature,
    nor does it attempt to teach you anything. It simply explains why this language exists.


    Let's start with a confession: Glos exists because other languages failed me.

    Not because they were poorly designed, or because their creators did not know what they
    were doing. Quite the opposite. Many are remarkable pieces of engineering, and I have
    learned a great deal from them.

    But somewhere along the way, I think software development lost something important.

    We have spent decades developing programming languages, frameworks, abstractions, build
    systems, package managers, design patterns, and guidelines. We have debated ownership,
    immutability, purity, object orientation, functional programming, and countless other
    ideas. We have found increasingly complicated ways to express very ordinary things.

    And somewhere in all this, we seem to have forgotten why we started programming.

    Most of us got into this field because we wanted to MAKE things. We wanted to write
    programs, solve problems, try ideas, and turn something in our imagination into
    something real.

    Yet building things has somehow become the last priority.

    Software projects have too many dependencies, too many tools, and too many layers.
    Simple programs require too much boilerplate. Sometimes building and configuring a
    project feels like a project of its own.

    We introduce abstractions even when the problem doesn't need them. And when an
    abstraction gets in the way, we add another one to work around it.

    We have become so used to fighting our tools that we consider it a normal part of
    programming.

    It doesn't have to be this way.


    There is another problem, too.

    The programming world has divided itself into camps. Imperative programming, functional
    programming, object-oriented programming, this language, that language, and so on.
    These were meant to be different ways of solving problems. Instead, they have become
    identities.

    You are either an OOP programmer or you are not. You are either part of the functional
    programming camp or you are not. You are expected to follow certain principles, use
    certain words, and defend a particular way of doing things.

    What was meant to be an instrument has become an identity. Imagine telling someone that
    you are an "if else" expert, or a "for loop" specialist. Doesn't it sound absurd?

    These are just tools. Use a struct when it makes sense. Use a function when it makes
    sense. Keep things immutable when that helps. Mutate when that is simpler. Use an
    abstraction when it makes the problem easier to solve, and leave it out when it doesn't.

    There is no prize for following a programming paradigm perfectly.


    This compiler is, in a sense, a result of that frustration.

    I have used many languages, and each has taught me something. I took the features I
    liked, left behind the ones I didn't, and tried to bring the good ideas together.

    The result is Glos.

    It is not an attempt to invent a new way of programming, or to prove that one language
    design is better than all others. It is simply my idea of an ideal systems programming
    language.

    A language that takes the intuitive semantics and good APIs of Go, the freedom and
    control of C, and useful ideas from other languages.

    A language that doesn't follow any particular dogma.

    A language where you can choose how memory is managed, work with C without unnecessary
    ceremony, use high-level features without losing low-level control, and write efficient
    programs without fighting the language.

    A language where things Just Work.


    PRAGMATISM

    If there is one word that describes the philosophy behind Glos, it is pragmatism.

    Not theoretical elegance. Not purity. Not safety at absolutely any cost.

    The important questions are simple:

        Does this solve a real problem?
        Does it make programming easier or more enjoyable?
        Is it worth the complexity it introduces?

    Safety, performance, and consistency all matter. But they are not goals in themselves.
    They are there to help us build better software.

    And that is the point: to build things, enjoy doing so, and get the job done.


    SOME ADVICE

    Write simple programs.

    Write code that gets the job done. Don't feel compelled to introduce patterns, layers,
    or abstractions just because they are considered good practice.

    Try things out. Experiment. Use whatever approach makes sense for the problem. Don't
    follow an ideal just because someone else says it is the right way to write software.

    Don't worry too much about styling. Consistency is useful, but it isn't the purpose of
    programming. Pick a reasonable style and stick to it. Don't spend more time debating
    formatting than solving the problem.

    Don't be afraid of repeating code. When you start working on a problem, you often don't
    know what abstractions will make sense. Instead of creating wrapper functions and
    structures too early, start with a simple solution, even if it is messy or repetitive.

    Once you have something that works, you will start to notice patterns. Perhaps the same
    group of statements appears in several places. Perhaps some variables are always passed
    together. Perhaps certain functions naturally belong with a particular data structure.

    These are good places to refactor. Let the code show you where abstractions are useful,
    instead of trying to guess them all beforehand.

    Of course, this doesn't mean you should never plan ahead. Just don't mistake designing
    a solution for understanding the problem.

    Write as little code as reasonably possible, but don't confuse brevity with readability.
    The goal isn't to minimize the number of characters. It is to minimize how much code
    someone has to understand.

    Prefer simple, separate statements over deeply nested expressions. Long expressions can
    be difficult to read, debug, and change. Sometimes a temporary variable and a few extra
    lines make things much clearer.

    Practise temperance.

    Just because the language supports operator overloading doesn't mean you should overload
    every operator. Keep it to cases where the meaning is obvious, such as mathematics and
    indexing. An operator should never surprise the reader.

    The same goes for 'first.glos'. It is useful for setting up the build environment,
    generating assets, and similar tasks. But don't turn it into an excuse to make an
    elaborate metaprogram.

    Keep the number of moving parts small. Every dependency, configuration file, and tool
    is another thing someone has to understand and maintain. Add them only when they are
    worth the trouble.

    Dependencies deserve particular care. They are code you don't control, and every one
    comes with a contract that you must keep up with. They can change, break, become
    abandoned, introduce bugs, or bring in requirements of their own.

    You cannot avoid all of this, but you can be careful about how much of it you take on.

    Don't write hundreds of lines when a handful will do. Don't build a framework when a
    function is enough. Don't write a library when a few definitions will suffice. When
    working with a C library, don't write bindings for functions you don't actually need
    just for the sake of completeness.

    Use assertions freely. The compile-time directive '#assert' has no runtime cost, and
    can catch entire classes of bugs. Don't avoid runtime assertions just to save a little
    performance, either. Your future self will thank you.

    Be willing to try different approaches. Just because something has always been done
    one way doesn't mean it is the best way. Try different APIs, algorithms, and designs.
    You might find something better.

    And above all, remain humble.

    Don't write clever code just to show that you can. Write code that other people,
    including your future self, can understand. Cleverness is no substitute for clarity.


    A WORD ON PERFORMANCE

    Of course, this doesn't mean clever code is always bad.

    Sometimes a simple implementation is too slow. You may need a better algorithm, a
    different data structure, or special treatment for a hot path.

    That's perfectly fine.

    But profile first.

    Don't make code harder to read for performance gains you haven't measured. Find the
    bottleneck, measure it, and optimize the part that actually matters.

    If you do write something clever for performance reasons, explain why. Document what
    it does and, if possible, include the measurements that justify it. You can also put
    it in a small inline function to keep the surrounding code readable.

    There is nothing wrong with clever code when there is a good reason for it.
    There is something wrong with writing it when there isn't.


    JOY OF PROGRAMMING

    Ultimately, all of this comes down to one thing.

    Programming should be enjoyable. You should be able to open your editor, write some
    code, and see your ideas come to life without having to fight your tools every step
    of the way.

    This doesn't mean programming will always be easy. Some problems are genuinely hard.
    Some algorithms are complicated. Some systems require a lot of care.

    But things should not be difficult without a good reason.

    That is what Glos hopes to achieve.

    It will not be perfect. No language is. There will be trade-offs, limitations, and
    decisions that some people disagree with. That's inevitable.

    Be pragmatic. Build things. Have fun.


    And finally, a small reminder.

    After all these years of programming language research, type systems, optimizations,
    and endless debates about the correct way to write software, there is still one program
    that we can all agree is perfect.

    It has no bugs. It has no unnecessary abstractions. It has no dependencies to manage.
    It needs no configuration, no build system, and no documentation.

    It simply says:

        Hello, world!

    Perhaps we should remember how to write programs like this.


    Now, go build something.

*/

main :: () {
    println("Hello, world!")
}
